WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.910
The universe is everything.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.429
There is nothing else, at least, as far as
we know.

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:13.980
There are theories that say there are other
universes and our universe is part of a much

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.420
larger multiverse.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.890
This very well may be true, but so far we
don't have strong enough evidence to back

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.210
up such theories.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:26.420
It is however, common knowledge that the universe
is expanding.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.760
This is undeniably true.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.720
Scientists have observed the distance between
large objects in space, such as galaxies and

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.600
have found that the distance between them
is increasing; they all seem to be moving

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.309
away from each other.

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:44.780
This leaves only one plausible explanation,
the universe is expanding.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.731
That means that all the time, new spacetime
is being created and the physical size of

00:00:49.731 --> 00:00:52.890
the universe is actually increasing.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.170
Just like blowing up a balloon.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.300
Let's just hope this one doesn't go and bloody
pop on us.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.829
So that leaves one inevitable, burning question.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.420
If the universe is expanding, what is it expanding
into?

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.460
Is the universe expanding into a much larger
multiverse like some astronomers say or are

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.130
we all inside a giant pigeon pie?

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:18.520
Let's find out...

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.570
The answer to this age-old question depends
entirely on whether the universe is truly

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.549
infinite or not.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:30.040
If you subscribe to the theory that the universe
is infinite then the answer to the question

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.799
"what is the universe expanding into?"

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:35.630
can only be "nothing".

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:40.430
Because if the universe is expanding, then
that means new spacetime is constantly being

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.430
added.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.390
It's just like the construction of new real
estate in space, but the universe doesn’t

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.330
have to apply for planning permission beforehand.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:54.049
This new real estate is a brand new area of
the universe that galaxies can occupy.

00:01:54.049 --> 00:02:01.560
But you can't add new space to an infinite
area because it's infinite, it continues forever.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.430
It has no end, no boundaries and no measurements.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:11.360
So if the universe is truly infinite then
the universe is expanding into nothing.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.190
It's as simple as that.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.560
Well it can be simple, if you sweep all that
bloody complex mathematics under the carpet

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.750
and just don't look at it.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.660
But most people have a really difficult time
understanding the concept of infinity.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.700
And we have an even harder time understanding
the concept of nothing.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.260
Just try and think about nothing for a second.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.910
You can't, it's impossible, you're thinking
about thinking about nothing, and that in

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.300
itself, is not nothing, it's something.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.300
Kittens!

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.920
And now you're thinking about kittens.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:50.771
My point is, the human brain isn't sufficiently
equipped to understand these concepts; it

00:02:50.771 --> 00:02:53.100
just doesn't have the necessary tools.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.950
But that's okay, because you may not need
to.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.260
Let's imagine for a minute that the universe
is finite, it has boundaries.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.760
It might be flat, round or some weird-ass
doughnut shape.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.780
But that doesn’t matter, let's just pretend
that we know for certain the universe has

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.730
an end and we can step beyond that wall and
see what's on the other side.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.900
By the way this is totally theoretical now
because the current, most widely accepted

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.080
notion within the scientific community is
that the universe is infinite.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.980
But that means the answer is just nothing,
and that's pretty boring, so let's just say

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.100
it's not.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.930
After all, the concept of an infinite universe
is just a theory, and theories can change

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.870
or be thrown out the window, at any moment.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:43.460
So the universe is finite and it has a boundary,
what's would be on the other side?

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.770
Well, you’ve really opened the floodgates
now, you've let all the wacky, weirdo nut-job

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:52.290
scientists with their theoretical physics
degrees and science fiction fetishes in and

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.340
they have endless theories.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.430
Seriously there are so many different answers
to this question you'll want to travel to

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.400
another dimension when you've read them all.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.370
But let's put our objectivity hats on and
take a look at a couple of the more popular

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.730
theories out there.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.640
One theory is that we are living inside a
black hole.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.720
Some believe that every time a new black hole
opens a new universe is created at the other

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:17.720
end of it.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.720
For a long time it has been theorised that
objects known as white holes exist and they

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.260
are essentially the complete opposite of black
holes.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.000
A black hole sucks in matter whereas a white
hole spews out matter.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.700
Like making a smoothie and leaving the lid
off the blender.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.670
To put it simply, shit gets messy.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:40.000
The theory states that anything that enters
a black hole eventually finds itself being

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.330
spewed out of a white hole somewhere completely
different within the spacetime continuum.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:51.020
But if white holes do exist they would be
extremely unstable and would only last for

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:57.630
a few seconds before disappearing, unlike
a black hole, which is considerably more stable.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:02.030
Some people believe that the big bang was
actually a white hole.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.560
Like an exit from a black hole within another
universe.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:11.120
Yep that's right, our entire universe and
everything we know, could have been created

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.840
as a result of a star dying and forming a
black hole in another universe or possibly

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.240
even just very, very far away in our own universe.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.310
Which would mean that we are living inside
a black hole and our universe is expanding

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.490
into an even larger, parent universe.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.320
But that's just one theory.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.530
Another popular theory is the "multiverse
theory" which states that our universe is

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:42.100
just one universe amongst many, other, parallel
universes.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.630
Like a stack of flat discs, and our entire
universe is contained inside just one of those

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:48.630
discs.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:53.810
If this is true then our universe is expanding
into whatever space is surrounding our universe

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.330
and our other neighbouring, parallel universes.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:04.790
Sadly the truth is, we currently have no idea
what, if anything lies beyond our universe.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:10.180
That's because if the universe is finite and
it does have a boundary, then we can't detect

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:16.440
that boundary because with our current technology
we can only see so far into space.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.320
This is known as the observable universe.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:25.430
The observable universe is a radius surrounding
us in every direction, which is ninety-three

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.710
billion light years across.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:33.020
The light from everything inside that sphere
has had enough time to reach our galaxy in

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:38.080
the 13.8 billion years that the universe has
been in existence.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.730
But the universe is expanding, so it's highly
probable that we will never be able to see

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:48.800
beyond the observable universe because objects
beyond the limits of the observable universe

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.410
are moving away from us.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:55.880
That means if the light from those objects
hasn't reached us by now, it never will.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:01.470
So we may never be able to detect the boundaries
of our universe, if they exist.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:07.550
And, unless we find that boundary, we can
never know for certain, what lies beyond.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:13.230
But science and technology is rapidly improving
and nobody knows what mind-blowing discoveries

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:18.520
that, previously seemed impossible, will become
very real in the near future.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.750
So always keep an open mind and never stop
asking questions, because if it weren’t

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:28.030
for that, then the human race wouldn't be
where it is today.

